WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.528 --> 00:00:03.179
(jazz music)

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:05.248
- New York's a tough city to live in.

00:00:05.248 --> 00:00:06.717
It always has been.

00:00:06.717 --> 00:00:08.961
Wait, see if we can hear them.

00:00:08.961 --> 00:00:10.353
The old saying "If you can make it here,

00:00:10.353 --> 00:00:11.913
"you can make it anywhere."

00:00:11.913 --> 00:00:14.613
I think because the
birds have made it here.

00:00:14.613 --> 00:00:17.079
I mean, they've endured this far.

00:00:17.079 --> 00:00:19.746
(birds calling)

00:00:22.354 --> 00:00:23.666
My name is Steve Baldwin,

00:00:23.666 --> 00:00:25.613
and I've been tracking the
wild parrots of Brooklyn

00:00:25.613 --> 00:00:27.503
for about 10 years now.

00:00:27.503 --> 00:00:29.373
Those two look sort of frisky.

00:00:29.373 --> 00:00:31.485
It's not really their mating season, but.

00:00:31.485 --> 00:00:33.055
Myiopsitta monachus.

00:00:33.055 --> 00:00:36.212
(birds screeching)

00:00:36.212 --> 00:00:37.562
The monk parakeet.

00:00:37.562 --> 00:00:39.663
Native to Argentina.

00:00:39.663 --> 00:00:41.482
And as far as we know,

00:00:41.482 --> 00:00:45.258
something happened at
Kennedy Airport in 1967

00:00:45.258 --> 00:00:46.828
or 1968.

00:00:46.828 --> 00:00:48.578
It appears that a crate full of parrots

00:00:48.578 --> 00:00:50.079
arrived from Argentina.

00:00:50.079 --> 00:00:52.349
Something went wrong, a
bunch of them escaped.

00:00:52.349 --> 00:00:55.791
They came to Brooklyn College, right here.

00:00:55.791 --> 00:00:58.871
We’ve got an incredible cast
of parrots here today.

00:00:58.871 --> 00:01:00.751
One, two, three, four.

00:01:00.751 --> 00:01:04.540
They're smaller than a pigeon,
but larger than a sparrow.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:05.831
They're the only kind of parrots

00:01:05.831 --> 00:01:08.660
that build these free-standing structures.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:13.350
These nests can get up to
the size of small cars.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.959
It's methodical work, it's
twig after twig after twig.

00:01:16.959 --> 00:01:20.419
Parrots are sort of the
primates of the bird world.

00:01:20.419 --> 00:01:22.471
They're smart; they're communicative;

00:01:22.471 --> 00:01:24.580
they eat almost anything
that's available to them.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.431
So they're perfectly set up, really,

00:01:26.431 --> 00:01:29.170
to thrive in a place like Brooklyn,

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:31.380
even though it's not where they came from.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.963
They've adapted extremely well.

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:38.229
You have to, I think, have
a certain fiber of steel

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:39.610
to make it in this town,

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:42.179
and I think the parrots
have those qualities.

00:01:42.179 --> 00:01:46.346
(upbeat music)
(parrots twittering)

